findings.body:
The new base size calls libc's `malloc_usable_size()` directly:

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1213-1217"}

The rest of Redis never does this. It always goes through `zmalloc_size()` / `zmalloc_usable_size()`, because the right function depends on the allocator. In `src/zmalloc.h`, `zmalloc_size` maps to `je_malloc_usable_size` (jemalloc), `tc_malloc_size` (tcmalloc), `malloc_size` (macOS), or `malloc_usable_size` (glibc libc builds only).

::callout{type="warning"}
The default Linux build uses jemalloc, which `deps/Makefile` configures with `--with-jemalloc-prefix=je_`. So `malloc_usable_size` here resolves to **glibc's** implementation and runs on a pointer jemalloc allocated. glibc reads a chunk header that doesn't exist, which is undefined behavior: you get garbage sizes or a segfault on every `MEMORY USAGE` call. On macOS the function isn't declared at all, which is a build failure (or an implicit-declaration error on GCC 14+). On builds without `HAVE_MALLOC_SIZE`, `o` points past zmalloc's `PREFIX_SIZE` header, so even libc gets an interior pointer.

Use the zmalloc abstraction, as the PR description itself says:

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1217-1217"}
    size_t asize = zmalloc_size((void *)o);

The extrapolation formula changed from per-element to per-node, but it still multiplies by the total element count:

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1233-1239"}

`elesize` is the sum of `sizeof(quicklistNode) + zmalloc_size(node->entry)` over the sampled *nodes*, so `elesize/samples` is the average size of one **node**. `ql->count` is the total number of **elements** across all nodes. Multiplying the two overestimates by about the average number of entries per node. With the default `list-max-listpack-size -2` (8KB nodes), a list of small items holds hundreds of entries per node, so MEMORY USAGE comes back hundreds of times too large.

The original `elesize/elecount*ql->count` was correct: average bytes per element times element count. (`ql->len * elesize/samples` would also work.) The list test only asserts `memory_usage > 0`, so it doesn't catch this.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1239-1239"}
            asize += (double)elesize/elecount*ql->count;

The comment above this block says we "use the average to compute the size of the first N-1 listpacks, and finally add the real size of the last node." The PR changed the multiplier from `numele-1` to `numele`, but the code still seeks to `$` and adds the last node's real size:

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1326-1338"}

So every stream with more listpack nodes than `sample_size` now counts one listpack twice. A listpack node can be up to `stream-node-max-bytes` (4KB by default), so the overestimate per stream is up to about 4KB. This isn't related to the kvobj fix and looks like an accidental regression. No test covers it.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1330-1330"}
            asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1);

The new string test is a good addition. However, the PR also rewrites the `asize` setup in every list/set/zset/hash/stream branch, and coverage for those is still only `assert_morethan [memory_usage k] 0` (see the renamed comments in hash.tcl/list.tcl). That's why the quicklist and stream regressions above get through.

::diff{file="tests/unit/type/string.tcl" lines="704-709"}

The INT branch also checks only a lower bound. A wrong base size (for example, the `malloc_usable_size` issue) that returns a large garbage value would still pass. Add an upper bound like the EMBSTR/RAW cases have. Also consider at least one bounded check for a multi-node quicklist and a multi-node stream.
